  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-3cd16b0f86eb675f": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-259",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "One part of the women probably sang, “Saul hath slain his thousands,” and the others responded, “and David his ten thousands.” This responsive chorus-singing is very ancient. Over four hundred years before this Miriam had led the women in the responsive chorus of victory on the o…"
      },
      "c-d9ce9954ecea7101": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-257",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "It is considered in the East a special mark of respect to be presented by a prince with some of the garments he has for his own wearing. The gift of a girdle is a token of the greatest confidence and affection, and is very highly prized. Joab expressed his intense desire for the…"
      },
      "c-edb8195b9668f78b": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-258",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "It came to pass as they came, when David was returned from the slaughter of the Philistine, that the women came out of all cities of Israel, singing and dancing, to meet king Saul, with tabrets, with joy, and with instruments of music."
      }
    },
